[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # ERRORS.md — mismatches found while differentially testing the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reference: `c_src/src/main.c`, built with CMake as `c_src/build/driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (gcc + glibc, x86-64 Linux).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Translation: `translation/src/main.rs`, built as `translation/target/release/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Method: both programs are run as subprocesses on identical stdin; stdout,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr and exit status are compared byte for byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`translation/tests/differential.rs`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program actually branches on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The program has a single input-dependent decision:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int x = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scanf("%d", &x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run(x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run(x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `run` is pure output plus mutation of the file-scope `the_house`, so every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input class is a class of the one `%d` conversion:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. EOF before any non-whitespace byte → no assignment, `x` stays `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. Matching failure (optional sign not followed by a digit) → no assignment,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `x` stays `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. Successful conversion in `int` range → `x` is that value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. Successful conversion out of `int` range but in `long` range → truncated to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `int` by the store through `int *`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. Conversion out of `long` range → glibc saturates (ERANGE) before truncating.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There are no other `if`s, no early `return`s, no length or null checks, and no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m path that writes to stderr or returns non-zero: `main` always `return 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — `scanf("%d")` wrapped instead of saturating on `long` overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Status: FIXED (`translation/src/main.rs`, `scanf_int`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The original Rust `scanf_int` accumulated the digit run into an `i64` using
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `wrapping_mul(10)` / `wrapping_add`, then cast to `i32`. glibc does not wrap.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Its `%d` directive converts through the same path as `strtol`: on overflow the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result saturates to `LONG_MAX` (or `LONG_MIN` for a negative sign) and `errno`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is set to `ERANGE`; that saturated `long` is then narrowed to `int` by plain
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m truncation when stored. On this platform `long` is 64-bit, so:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - overflowing positive input → `(int)LONG_MAX` → `-1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - overflowing negative input → `(int)LONG_MIN` → `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed divergence (value of `x` inferred from the `bedrooms` field, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m starts at `5` and has `x` added to it once per `run` call):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | C `x` | Rust `x` (before fix) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `99999999999999999999` | `-1` | `1661992959` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-99999999999999999999` | `0` | `-1661992959` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `18446744073709551616` (2^64) | `-1` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1` repeated 40 times | `-1` | `-1908874354` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rendered difference for stdin `99999999999999999999` (line 4 of stdout):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:    The house has 3 floors, 4 bedrooms, and 3.5 bathrooms
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: The house has 3 floors, 1661992964 bedrooms, and 3.5 bathrooms
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: accumulate the magnitude in a `u64` with `checked_mul` / `checked_add`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m latch an `overflow` flag when it no longer fits, compare against the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign-dependent limit (`LONG_MAX` for `+`, `-LONG_MIN` for `-`), yield
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i64::MAX` / `i64::MIN` when out of range, and only then cast to `i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that wrapping and saturating agree for inputs that overflow `int` but
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m still fit in `long` (`2147483648` → `-2147483648`, `4294967296` → `0`), so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m those cases did not expose the bug; only inputs beyond `long` range did. Tests
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `values_beyond_int_are_truncated_from_long` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `values_beyond_long_saturate_then_truncate` now cover both sides of that edge.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — test harness deadlock/EPIPE on large stdin (not a translation bug)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Status: FIXED in the test harness only; no change to `src/main.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The first version of the harness wrote stdin from the test thread and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `expect()`ed the write to succeed. Neither program drains stdin before exiting,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so a payload larger than the pipe buffer (~64 KiB) makes the write fail with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `BrokenPipe` once the child has exited. This is identical behavior for the C and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the Rust binary and is not observable in stdout/stderr/status, so the harness now
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writes stdin on a helper thread and treats `BrokenPipe` as expected. The helper
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m thread also removes the possibility of deadlocking against the child's captured
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout/stderr pipes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The harness additionally runs every case twice — once with stdin as a pipe and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m once with stdin as a regular file redirect — because a seekable stdin is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m different stdio configuration than a pipe.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors verified as already matching (no fix needed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `%.1f` formatting of `bathrooms`. It only ever takes the values `2.5`, `3.5`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and `4.5`, all exactly representable, so no rounding-mode difference between
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   glibc and Rust's `{:.1}` can surface. The NaN/infinity handling in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `format_f1` is unreachable from any input but is harmless.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `%d` leading-whitespace skip, including across newlines (`scanf` semantics,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   unlike `fgets`): `"\n\n\n42"` and `"   42"` both yield `42`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Whitespace-only and empty input both leave `x == 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Matching failure cases: `"abc"`, `"-"`, `"+"`, `"--5"`, `"+ 5"`, `".5"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"e5"` — all leave `x == 0` and still exit `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Conversion stops at the first non-digit: `"12abc"` → `12`, `"0x10"` → `0`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"1e5"` → `1`, `"5.5"` → `5`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Leading zeros are decimal, not octal, and a long run of them does not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   trigger the overflow path: `"007"` → `7`, 5000 zeros then `7` → `7`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Signed wraparound of `bedrooms` (`int`) across the two `run` calls, e.g.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `2147483647` → `-2147483644` then `3`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `-0` → `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Binary stdin: leading NUL, bytes `0x80`–`0xff`, all 256 byte values,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   invalid UTF-8. Non-digit, non-whitespace bytes are a matching failure in both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Digit runs and whitespace runs straddling the Rust reader's 4096-byte
