[1mThe one real translation bug[22m was in [38;5;10mscanf_int[0m: it accumulated the digit run with [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m on an [38;5;10mi64[0m. glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m goes through [38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics — on out-of-range input it [3msaturates[23m to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m (setting [38;5;10mERANGE[0m), and that saturated [38;5;10mlong[0m is then truncated to [38;5;10mint[0m by the store through [38;5;10mint *[0m. So [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m gives [38;5;10mx == -1[0m in C, but the Rust gave [38;5;10m1661992959[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This was invisible on the obvious overflow tests: for inputs that overflow [38;5;10mint[0m but still fit in [38;5;10mlong[0m ([38;5;10m2147483648[0m, [38;5;10m4294967296[0m), wrapping and saturating agree. Only inputs past [38;5;10mlong[0m range expose it. The fix accumulates the magnitude in [38;5;10mu64[0m with [38;5;10mchecked_*[0m, latches an overflow flag, compares against the sign-dependent limit, and yields [38;5;10mi64::MAX[0m/[38;5;10mi64::MIN[0m before the [38;5;10mas i32[0m cast.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mInput classes enumerated from the C.[22m The program has exactly one input-dependent decision — the single [38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m. There are no other [38;5;10mif[0ms, no early returns, no null or length checks, and no path that writes stderr or returns non-zero. So the branch classes are: EOF before any token, matching failure (sign with no digit), in-[38;5;10mint[0m-range, out-of-[38;5;10mint[0m-but-in-[38;5;10mlong[0m, and out-of-[38;5;10mlong[0m. Covered alongside those: whitespace skipping across newlines, conversion stopping at the first non-digit, decimal-not-octal leading zeros, signed wraparound of [38;5;10mbedrooms[0m across the two [38;5;10mrun[0m calls, binary/invalid-UTF-8 stdin, and digit runs straddling the reader's 4096-byte buffer edge.[0m[0m
